 @Dempig   for one, assassins creed is a somewhat demanding aaa title with a built in benchmarking feature, which by the way had a 1% low of 70 fps, and it’s comparable to control and far cry 6. But if  you want to bitch about those games, look up benchmarks online and stop trying to argue with nothing but spite. According to a far cry 6 benchmark from Tom’s Hardware surveying over 20 different cards, the 3070 performed with an average of 100 fps at 1440p ultra, a far higher average than the 3060 at 71 fps. For a card you claim to be massively inferior to the 3060 due to vram and not even processing power, you are flat-out wrong. But maybe it was just a fluke. Let’s check the benchmarks for control, another title you recommended, at 1440p max settings, rtx off. It reached 83.3 fps as an average, with a 1% low of 69.1 fps. Again, not exactly terrible performance. In both cases, the rtx 3060 12gb performed lower. And while yes, the two get pretty close at 1080p, your argument was the vram would bottleneck at 4k. However, even at 4k ultra in far cry 6, the 3070 had an 18-20 fps lead over the 3060 with its “superior” vram, which is the exact opposite of what you were trying to say.

But if statistics aren’t enough for you, how about plain logic? Your argument claims that the 3070 is unable to load textures at higher resolutions. Not to get too technical as you clearly lack any in-depth understanding of how GPUs work, but that statement in itself is wrong. Any modern system and its graphics card won’t “flat out not load textures” when it hits its memory buffer limit. Instead, it sends the textures to your system RAM and loads them from there. This obviously causes slowdowns and fps drops, but none of that missing texture bullshit you claim, which is an entirely different problem that your apparently prehistoric rig must have, since modern PCs just don’t do that nowadays. Instead what would happen would be small frame drops every time a new scene loads in or you move the camera fast enough, which would manifest itself as lower 1% lows on the benchmark. However, as I previously stated, the 3070 had significantly higher lows than the 3060, and the average-to-low fps ratio was actually about the same between the two, despite the 3060 having higher vram. This is due to the fact that the 3070 has a 256-bit bus compared to the 3060’s 192-bit bus, meaning the 3070 can transfer data in and out of vram faster, meaning less data may need to be stored in vram at one time. This can be proven by my old r9 390, which has 8 gigs as well but often hit vram limits because the memory itself was far slower. But I digress.

So no, the 3070 isn’t “junk”, and no mine isn’t special, that’s all of them. Literally everything on the internet will tell you that it outperforms the 3060 12gb, but that should’ve been obvious. It even outperforms the 4060 much of the time, which was my original point in posting this comment that you chose to turn into a 3070-trashing session. I’m not trying to fanboy over the 3070 (they definitely could’ve increased the vram capacity but chose to be cheap, as NVIDIA always does), and I’m not claiming it’s the best card to buy here (amd arguably makes better competitors for the price). These “points” you make sound more like either a faulty card you were sent, or a bottleneck or other issue with the rest of your system (possibly too much power draw for your psu to handle). Please research before you try and make an argument based off your opinion on the internet, where anyone can find factual information on a topic.

